WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.949
Radiometric thermal sensors record measurement data for pixels or defined areas within stated limits.

00:00:05.949 --> 00:00:08.874
Non-radiometric sensors primarily provide relative visual contrast.

00:00:08.874 --> 00:00:10.545
Both can support observation,

00:00:10.545 --> 00:00:15.559
but only appropriate radiometric systems should be used for qualified temperature measurement.

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:17.230
Resolution affects spatial detail,

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:23.498
while thermal sensitivity describes the sensor's ability to distinguish small temperature differences under specified conditions.

00:00:23.498 --> 00:00:25.587
Refresh rate affects motion rendering.

00:00:25.587 --> 00:00:29.347
None of these specifications alone guarantees a useful inspection.

00:00:29.347 --> 00:00:33.107
Sensitivity is not the same as absolute measurement accuracy.

00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:38.957
A sensor may display subtle contrast while the numerical value still depends on calibration,

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:41.882
target coverage, emissivity assumptions, reflected radiation, range,

00:00:41.882 --> 00:00:43.135
and environmental conditions.

00:00:43.135 --> 00:00:47.313
Lens field of view controls scene coverage and target size.

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:53.163
A wider view covers more area but places fewer pixels on a small target.

00:00:53.163 --> 00:00:59.012
A narrower view can improve target detail from an appropriate distance while reducing context.

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:02.772
Calibration supports consistency between detector response and reported values.

00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:07.368
Some systems perform internal correction automatically, but calibration status, warm-up, range,

00:01:07.368 --> 00:01:09.457
and environmental assumptions still matter.

00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:11.546
Follow manufacturer and organizational procedures.

00:01:11.546 --> 00:01:15.307
Drone-mounted payloads add vibration, movement, changing angle, altitude, wind,

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.560
and flight-safety constraints.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.738
The aircraft must carry the payload safely, preserve required clearance,

00:01:20.738 --> 00:01:24.499
and collect enough stable target pixels for the mission.

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:26.588
Match capability to the question.

00:01:26.588 --> 00:01:31.602
Screening for broad anomalies, measuring a small electrical connection, mapping a roof,

00:01:31.602 --> 00:01:35.780
and supporting fire awareness have different sensor, lens, distance, timing,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.033
and expertise requirements.

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:39.540
Define acceptable uncertainty before selecting equipment.
